  content: `
  <p>Renting a car can be a great way to explore Europe, especially when your itinerary includes smaller towns, scenic routes, countryside, or multiple destinations.</p>

  <p>However, driving in Europe can involve different road rules, toll systems, parking regulations, and rental conditions depending on the countries you visit.</p>

  <p>Before booking, check these eight important things to make your European road trip easier.</p>

  <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1473445361085-b9a07f55608b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80" alt="Scenic European road trip through the countryside" class="in-article-img" />

  <h2>🪪 Check Your Driving Licence Requirements</h2>

  <p>Before renting a car, check the driving licence requirements for every country on your itinerary.</p>

  <p>Requirements can vary depending on your nationality, licence, destination, and rental company.</p>

  <p>If you are traveling with a licence issued outside Europe, check whether you need an international driving permit or additional documentation before your trip.</p>

  <p>Do not wait until you reach the rental counter to find out that additional documents are required.</p>

  <h2>🛡️ Understand Rental Insurance</h2>

  <p>Review the protection included with your rental before booking.</p>

  <p>Check how damage and theft are handled, what the excess or deductible is, and which situations may be excluded.</p>

  <p>If you already have insurance or other rental coverage, check its terms to make sure it applies to your destination and rental vehicle.</p>

  <p>European countries do not all have identical insurance requirements, so check the specific rental conditions for your trip.</p>

  <h2>🛣️ Learn About Tolls and Road Charges</h2>

  <p>Road tolls are an important part of driving in some European countries.</p>

  <p>Depending on where you travel, you may encounter toll booths, electronic toll systems, road-use charges, or required motorway permits or vignettes.</p>

  <p>Check the countries on your route and understand how their road charges work before starting your journey.</p>

  <p>Also check whether your rental vehicle already has the equipment or registration needed for electronic toll roads.</p>

  <h2>🅿️ Research Parking Before You Arrive</h2>

  <p>Parking can be one of the biggest challenges when driving through European cities.</p>

  <p>Historic city centers may have limited parking, restricted driving zones, or areas where vehicles are not permitted.</p>

  <p>Before arriving, check whether your hotel has parking and whether your destination has any restricted traffic zones.</p>

  <p>In some cities, leaving the car outside the center and using public transport may be easier than driving directly into the city.</p>

  <h2>⛽ Check the Fuel Policy</h2>

  <p>Read the rental company's fuel policy before collecting the vehicle.</p>

  <p>Check the starting fuel level, required return level, and how fuel charges are handled.</p>

  <p>Also confirm what type of fuel the vehicle uses and understand the local fuel station system.</p>

  <p>If your trip crosses several countries, remember that fuel prices can vary significantly between locations.</p>

  <h2>🌍 Check Whether You Can Cross Borders</h2>

  <p>If you are planning to visit more than one European country, check the rental company's cross-border rules before booking.</p>

  <p>Not every rental vehicle or rental agreement automatically allows travel into every neighboring country.</p>

  <p>Additional documentation, fees, insurance conditions, or restrictions may apply depending on the countries involved.</p>

  <p>Tell the rental company about your intended route and confirm that cross-border travel is permitted before you leave the pickup location.</p>

  <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
    "A European road trip is easier when you check the rules for every country on your route—not just the country where you collect the car."
  </blockquote>

  <h2>🚗 Choose the Right Car</h2>

  <p>Vehicle size matters more than you might expect on a European road trip.</p>

  <p>A smaller car can be easier to drive and park in narrow streets and older city centers, while a larger vehicle may provide more comfort and luggage space.</p>

  <p>Think about the number of passengers, luggage, road conditions, parking, and the destinations on your itinerary before choosing your rental category.</p>

  <h2>📱 Check Navigation and Driving Rules</h2>

  <p>Make sure you have reliable navigation before starting your journey.</p>

  <p>It can also help to learn the basic road signs, speed limits, parking rules, and driving requirements for the countries you will visit.</p>

  <p>If your route includes mountainous areas or winter travel, check whether seasonal equipment or other requirements apply to your journey.</p>

  <h2>🧳 Europe Car Rental Checklist</h2>

  <div class="flight-tips-card">
    <h3>🚗 Before Booking</h3>

    <p><strong>Licence:</strong> Check driving licence and international permit requirements.</p>

    <p><strong>Insurance:</strong> Understand coverage, exclusions, and the excess or deductible.</p>

    <p><strong>Tolls:</strong> Check motorway tolls, electronic systems, and road-use requirements.</p>

    <p><strong>Parking:</strong> Research parking and restricted driving areas.</p>

    <p><strong>Fuel:</strong> Understand the rental company's fuel policy.</p>

    <p><strong>Border crossings:</strong> Confirm that your rental can be driven into every country on your route.</p>

    <p><strong>Vehicle:</strong> Choose a car that suits European roads, parking, passengers, and luggage.</p>

    <p><strong>Rules:</strong> Learn the important driving requirements for your destinations.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>💰 Compare the Complete Rental Cost</h2>

  <p>Do not compare European rental cars only by their advertised daily rate.</p>

  <p>Consider insurance, deposits, mileage, fuel, additional drivers, airport fees, one-way charges, cross-border fees, and other applicable costs.</p>

  <p>A slightly more expensive rental may offer better overall value if it includes services you actually need.</p>

  <h2>✈️ Compare European Car Rentals With FlySava</h2>

  <p>Renting a car can give you much more freedom during a European trip, but preparation is important.</p>

  <p>Before booking, check the driving requirements, rental conditions, insurance, tolls, parking, fuel policy, and cross-border rules for your itinerary.</p>

  <p><strong>FlySava</strong> helps you plan more of your trip in one place, including flights, hotels, car rentals, flight status, eSIM options, and destination travel information.</p>

  <div class="flight-tips-card">
    <h3>🧳 Before Your European Road Trip</h3>

    <p><strong>Check:</strong> Licence and documentation requirements.</p>
    <p><strong>Review:</strong> Insurance and rental protection.</p>
    <p><strong>Plan:</strong> Tolls, parking, fuel, and road charges.</p>
    <p><strong>Confirm:</strong> Cross-border rental permissions.</p>
    <p><strong>Choose:</strong> A vehicle suited to your route and luggage.</p>
    <p><strong>Compare:</strong> The complete rental cost before booking.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Can I rent a car in one European country and drive to another?</summary>
    <p>It may be possible, but cross-border rules vary by rental company, vehicle, pickup country, and destination country. Confirm that your planned route is permitted before booking.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Do I need an international driving permit to rent a car in Europe?</summary>
    <p>It depends on your licence, nationality, destination, and rental company. Check the requirements for the specific countries you plan to visit before your trip.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Are European rental cars allowed to cross borders?</summary>
    <p>Some rentals allow cross-border travel, while others may restrict certain countries or require additional documentation or fees. Always check the rental agreement.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Are toll roads common in Europe?</summary>
    <p>Many European countries have toll roads or other road-use charges, but the systems differ by country. Check the toll requirements for your specific route.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Is a small car better for driving in Europe?</summary>
    <p>A smaller vehicle can be easier to park and maneuver in narrow streets and older city centers. However, choose a vehicle based on your passengers, luggage, route, and comfort needs.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>What should I check before renting a car in Europe?</summary>
    <p>Check driving licence requirements, insurance, deposit, mileage, fuel policy, tolls, parking, cross-border rules, vehicle size, and the complete rental price.</p>
  </details>

  <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

  <p>Renting a car can be one of the best ways to explore Europe, particularly when your trip includes multiple destinations or places that are difficult to reach by public transport.</p>

  <p>But every country can have different driving rules and rental conditions. Check the requirements for your entire route before booking.</p>

  <p><strong>Compare car rentals with FlySava and prepare for your European road trip with the right vehicle and rental terms.</strong></p>
`
