  content: `
  <p>When booking accommodation, the words <strong>hotel</strong> and <strong>resort</strong> can sometimes seem interchangeable. Both provide a place to sleep, but the experience they offer can be quite different.</p>

  <p>A hotel is generally focused on accommodation and the services you need during your stay. A resort is typically designed to be more of a complete destination, with facilities and activities that encourage guests to spend much of their time on the property.</p>

  <p>Neither is automatically better. The right choice depends on what you want from the trip.</p>

  <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/297985/pexels-photo-297985.jpeg" alt="Luxury resort-style accommodation with swimming pool" class="in-article-img" />

  <h2>What Is a Hotel?</h2>

  <p>A hotel primarily provides accommodation along with services and facilities designed to make your stay comfortable.</p>

  <p>Hotels can range from simple budget properties to luxury city hotels. Some offer restaurants, gyms, pools, meeting rooms, spas, or other facilities, but these aren't necessarily the main reason guests choose the property.</p>

  <p>Location is often one of the biggest advantages of a traditional hotel.</p>

  <p>You might choose a hotel because it is close to a city center, airport, business district, railway station, beach, or the attractions you want to visit.</p>

  <h2>What Is a Resort?</h2>

  <p>A resort is generally designed to provide more of the vacation experience within the property itself.</p>

  <p>Instead of simply being a place to stay while you explore a destination, a resort may offer restaurants, swimming pools, spas, beaches, sports facilities, entertainment, children's activities, or organized excursions.</p>

  <p>The exact facilities vary considerably from one resort to another.</p>

  <p>Some are focused on beaches and relaxation, while others are built around skiing, golf, wellness, family activities, or outdoor adventures.</p>

  <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
    "A hotel is often your base for exploring a destination. A resort can be part of the destination itself."
  </blockquote>

  <h2>The Biggest Difference: How You Spend Your Time</h2>

  <p>This is probably the simplest way to understand the difference.</p>

  <p>If you plan to leave your accommodation every morning and spend the day exploring museums, neighborhoods, restaurants, and attractions, a hotel may be all you need.</p>

  <p>If you want to spend much of your vacation swimming, relaxing by the pool, using the spa, enjoying on-site restaurants, or taking part in activities, a resort may be a better fit.</p>

  <div class="flight-tips-card">
    <h3>Think About Your Itinerary</h3>

    <p><strong>Exploring a city:</strong> A hotel can give you a convenient base close to the places you want to visit.</p>

    <p><strong>Relaxing by the beach:</strong> A resort may offer more of the facilities you want without needing to leave.</p>

    <p><strong>Family vacation:</strong> Resorts can provide pools, activities, and entertainment designed for different age groups.</p>

    <p><strong>Business trip:</strong> A hotel near your meetings or workplace may be more practical.</p>

    <p><strong>Wellness getaway:</strong> A resort with spa and wellness facilities may fit the purpose of the trip better.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Location Is Often Different</h2>

  <p>Hotels are commonly found in areas where travelers need convenient access to transportation, businesses, attractions, and city centers.</p>

  <p>Resorts are often located in destinations where the surrounding environment is part of the experience—such as beaches, mountains, islands, countryside, or large recreational areas.</p>

  <p>That isn't a strict rule. You can find resorts in cities and hotels in remote destinations.</p>

  <p>The useful distinction is whether the property's location and facilities are designed mainly to support exploration outside the property or activities within it.</p>

  <h2>Facilities: Hotel vs Resort</h2>

  <p>A hotel may provide the essentials you need for a comfortable stay, while a resort often offers a wider range of leisure facilities.</p>

  <p>Depending on the property, a resort might include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>🏊 Multiple swimming pools</li>
    <li>🏖️ Private or direct beach access</li>
    <li>💆 Spa and wellness facilities</li>
    <li>🍽️ Multiple restaurants and bars</li>
    <li>🎾 Sports facilities</li>
    <li>🎭 Entertainment and activities</li>
    <li>👨‍👩‍👧‍👦 Children's clubs or family activities</li>
    <li>🏌️ Golf or other recreational facilities</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Hotels can offer many of these features too, particularly at the luxury end of the market. The difference is usually the overall purpose and scale of the property.</p>

  <h2>Why Resorts Can Cost More</h2>

  <p>A resort can have a higher room rate because you are potentially paying for access to a much wider range of facilities and services.</p>

  <p>However, the nightly price alone doesn't tell you whether a resort is expensive for your particular trip.</p>

  <p>If you would otherwise spend money on restaurants, activities, transportation, pool access, spa treatments, or entertainment elsewhere, some of those costs may already be part of the resort experience.</p>

  <p>On the other hand, if you plan to spend every day outside the property, paying for facilities you won't use may not make financial sense.</p>

  <h2>All-Inclusive Doesn't Automatically Mean Resort</h2>

  <p>It's easy to confuse the terms <strong>resort</strong> and <strong>all-inclusive</strong>, but they describe different things.</p>

  <p>A resort describes the type of property and experience.</p>

  <p>All-inclusive describes how certain meals, drinks, activities, and services are packaged into the booking price.</p>

  <p>Some resorts are all-inclusive, while others charge separately for meals and activities.</p>

  <p>Always check exactly what is included before assuming that a resort rate covers everything.</p>

  <h2>Which Is Better for Families?</h2>

  <p>Families often appreciate resorts because they can provide several activities within one property.</p>

  <p>A pool, children's activities, restaurants, sports facilities, and entertainment can reduce the need to plan separate activities every day.</p>

  <p>But a hotel can still be a better choice for families who plan to spend most of their time exploring a city or visiting attractions outside the property.</p>

  <p>In that situation, location and room configuration may matter more than the number of facilities.</p>

  <h2>Which Is Better for Couples?</h2>

  <p>It depends on the type of getaway.</p>

  <p>A resort can work well for couples looking for relaxation, beaches, spa treatments, fine dining, or a more self-contained vacation.</p>

  <p>A city hotel may be better for a couple who wants to spend their days exploring neighborhoods, museums, restaurants, nightlife, and local culture.</p>

  <p>For a romantic trip, think about the experience you want rather than choosing based on the label alone.</p>

  <h2>What About Business Travelers?</h2>

  <p>For a business trip, a traditional hotel is often more practical.</p>

  <p>Being close to offices, conference centers, airports, or public transportation can be more valuable than having extensive leisure facilities.</p>

  <p>That said, some resorts also cater to business events and conferences, so the distinction depends on the specific property.</p>

  <h2>Look at the Total Cost</h2>

  <p>Just as with any hotel booking, don't compare properties using the room rate alone.</p>

  <p>Check what is included and what costs extra.</p>

  <p>At a resort, you may want to check for additional charges related to meals, activities, spa services, parking, transportation, or resort-specific fees.</p>

  <p>At a traditional hotel, consider breakfast, parking, transportation, and other services you may need during your stay.</p>

  <div class="flight-tips-card">
    <h3>A Better Cost Comparison</h3>

    <p><strong>Room:</strong> What does the nightly rate include?</p>

    <p><strong>Meals:</strong> Are breakfast or other meals included?</p>

    <p><strong>Activities:</strong> Are facilities and activities included or charged separately?</p>

    <p><strong>Transport:</strong> Will you need taxis, public transport, or a rental car?</p>

    <p><strong>Fees:</strong> Are there additional mandatory charges?</p>

    <p><strong>Location:</strong> How much will you spend getting to the places you want to visit?</p>
  </div>

  <h2>A Hotel Can Be the Better Resort</h2>

  <p>Sometimes the best accommodation for a relaxing vacation isn't technically called a resort.</p>

  <p>A boutique hotel with a beautiful pool, excellent restaurant, spa, and quiet surroundings can provide a resort-like experience without being classified as a resort.</p>

  <p>That's why it's better to compare the actual facilities and experience rather than relying entirely on the property's category.</p>

  <h2>How to Choose Between Them</h2>

  <p>Start with your itinerary.</p>

  <p>If you already have a long list of attractions and activities outside your accommodation, prioritize location, transport, comfort, and price.</p>

  <p>If your ideal vacation involves spending significant time at the property itself, look closely at the resort's facilities, dining options, activities, and included services.</p>

  <p>In other words, choose the accommodation that supports the way you actually want to spend your vacation.</p>

  <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>

  <div class="flight-tips-card">
    <h3>Hotel or Resort?</h3>

    <p><strong>Hotel:</strong> Usually a practical base for exploring a destination.</p>

    <p><strong>Resort:</strong> Often designed as a destination experience with more on-site leisure facilities.</p>

    <p><strong>Hotel:</strong> Often better suited to city breaks, business trips, and sightseeing-focused vacations.</p>

    <p><strong>Resort:</strong> Often better suited to relaxation, beach holidays, family vacations, and activity-focused stays.</p>

    <p><strong>Hotel:</strong> You may spend more time outside the property.</p>

    <p><strong>Resort:</strong> You may spend much of your vacation within the property.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Compare the Experience, Not Just the Label</h2>

  <p>The difference between a hotel and a resort is ultimately about what the property is designed to offer.</p>

  <p>A hotel can be the perfect choice when you want a comfortable and convenient base. A resort can be better when you want accommodation, recreation, dining, and relaxation to come together in one place.</p>

  <p>When comparing properties on FlySava, look at the actual facilities, location, total price, included services, and guest experience rather than choosing simply because one is called a resort.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>What is the main difference between a hotel and a resort?</summary>
    <p>A hotel primarily provides accommodation and related services, while a resort is generally designed to offer a broader vacation experience with more leisure facilities and activities on the property.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Are resorts more expensive than hotels?</summary>
    <p>Not necessarily. Resorts can have higher room rates because of their facilities, but the overall value depends on what is included and how much you would otherwise spend on meals, activities, and transportation.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Is a resort always all-inclusive?</summary>
    <p>No. Some resorts are all-inclusive, while others charge separately for meals, drinks, activities, and services. Check the individual property's rate conditions.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Is a hotel or resort better for a family vacation?</summary>
    <p>It depends on the trip. A resort can be convenient when you want pools, activities, entertainment, and dining in one place. A hotel may be better if your family plans to spend most of the time exploring the destination.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Is a resort better for a relaxing vacation?</summary>
    <p>Often, yes. Resorts frequently offer facilities such as pools, spas, restaurants, beaches, and organized activities that make it easy to relax without leaving the property.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Can a hotel offer resort-style facilities?</summary>
    <p>Yes. Some hotels have extensive pools, spas, restaurants, recreational facilities, and other amenities. Compare the actual property rather than relying only on whether it is classified as a hotel or resort.</p>
  </details>

  <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

  <p>Choose a <strong>hotel</strong> when you mainly need a comfortable, convenient base for exploring a destination.</p>

  <p>Choose a <strong>resort</strong> when the accommodation itself is an important part of the vacation and you want more activities, dining, relaxation, and entertainment on-site.</p>

  <p><strong>The best choice isn't about which one sounds more luxurious. It's about which one fits the trip you actually want to take.</strong></p>
`
